          %title Multiagent systems
          %h1#multiagent_systems Multiagent systems
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#introduction"} Introduction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#models_for_these_logics"} Models for these logics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#interpreted_systems"} Interpreted systems
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#wikipedia"} Wikipedia
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#books"} Books
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#articles"} Articles
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p To quote from a book on the subject:
          %blockquote
            %p
              “Multiagent systems are a new paradigm for understanding and building distributed systems, where it is assumed that the computational components are autonomous: able to control their own behaviour in the furtherance of their own goals.” (
              %a{:href => "#Wooldridge09"}> Wooldridge09
              )
          %p
            The area is a very wide one and this entry only deals with some generalities especially links between local ‘knowledge’ and more global forms and the models of this that involve the use of various types of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            In many studies of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributed+system"} distributed system
          %ul
            %li An agent is a processor, sensor or finite state machine, interconnected by a communication network with other ‘agents’.
          %p
            Typically each agent has a local state that is a function of its initial state, the messages received from other agents, observations of the external environment and possible internal actions. It has become customary when using formal models of distributed systems to use modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemic+logics"} epistemic logics
            as one of the tools for studying the knowledge of such systems. The basic such logic for handling a system with
            = succeed "-agents" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+S5%28m%29"} S5(n)
          %p
            For instance, the way the various agents are connected influences the logic in subtle ways. Suppose that agent 1 sends all its information immediately to agents 2 and 3, then if we denote by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            ‘knows’ proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 3
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1 \phi \Rightarrow K_2\phi \wedge K_3 \phi,
          %p in other words, if 1 knows something then we have that both 2 and 3 know it.
          %p
            The logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mn 5
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S5_n
            is obtained from ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            by adding ‘knowledge operators’,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi i
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i\phi
            is often replaced by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mi i
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i\phi
            %em ideal
            knowledge agents who have the properties of
          %ul
            %li
              %p veridical knowledge (everything they know is true),
            %li
              %p positive introspection (they know what they know)
          %p and
          %ul
            %li negative introspection (they know what they do not know).
          %p
            These properties are reflected in the axiom system for the logic. For more on this see the entry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+S5%28m%29"} S5(n)
          %h2#models_for_these_logics Models for these logics
          %p
            The classical models for multimodal logics, and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mn 5
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S5_n
            and its extensions in particular, are combinatorial models known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+frames"} Kripke frames
            and, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mn 5
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S5_n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "," do
              %em set of possible worlds
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_i
            is that if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_2
            are two possible worlds and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_1\sim_i w_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            cannot tell these two worlds apart.
          %h2#interpreted_systems Interpreted systems
          %p
            Fagin, Halpern, Moses and Vardi, in various combinations, have put forward a simpler combinatorial model known as an
            = succeed "." do
              %em interpreted system
          %p
            As before one has a set,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \{1,2, \ldots, n\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            can be in any state of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_i
            of local states. In addition one assumes given a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_e
            of possible states of the `environment'.  More formally:
